  {
   "post_type": "answer",
   "post_id": 6059024,
   "parent_id": 6058909,
   "author_user_id": 298661,
   "author_display_name": "Puppy",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": false,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/a/6059024",
   "score": 2,
   "created_utc": "2011-05-19T13:00:29Z",
   "last_edit_utc": null,
   "last_activity_utc": "2011-05-19T13:00:29Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "<p>It's very unlikely that such a thing would happen if you design the ownerships in advance and use smart pointers to enforce them if necessary. I doubt that they'll do anything but throw on a generic bad deallocation.</p>\n"
  },
  {
   "post_type": "answer",
   "post_id": 6059182,
   "parent_id": 6058909,
   "author_user_id": 1560850,
   "author_display_name": "valdo",
   "author_id_missing": false,
   "role": "responder",
   "is_accepted": true,
   "permalink": "https://stackoverflow.com/a/6059182",
   "score": 2,
   "created_utc": "2011-05-19T13:12:01Z",
   "last_edit_utc": null,
   "last_activity_utc": "2011-05-19T13:12:01Z",
   "license": "CC BY-SA 3.0",
   "body_html": "<p>Most of the heap implementations are not tolerate to invalid pointers (i.e. when you <code>delete</code> an address which was not returned to you by the heap). Almost for sure standard windows heap, and CRT's heap (implemented by MSVC) cause a debug breakpoint in such a case.</p>\n\n<p>You may also replace the implementation of <code>new</code>/<code>delete</code> operators and do the checking yourself (in your case you only want to check if the address belongs to the stack memory, which is easy).</p>\n"
  },
  {
   "post_type": "comment",
   "post_id": 7013249,
   "parent_id": 6059024,
